00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:05.480
way?

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:08.510
Speaker 6: Well, let's start by understanding
why you behave that way, which is because

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:10.750
your brain is wired to behave that way.

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:17.389
OK, so if we think about how do children learn
language, how do children learn body like

00:05:17.389 --> 00:05:18.389
body movements?

00:05:18.389 --> 00:05:23.700
And if we think about as our language evolves
over time, you know, we start to use new slang.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:28.660
It's because our brain is literally wired
where communal animals is Homo sapiens.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:33.110
And we look at what the other Homo sapiens
are doing and we are going to feel like doing

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:34.210
the same thing.

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:35.670
It's why peer pressure is a thing.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:37.300
It's why fashion fads are thing.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.770
It's just the way that we're wired now.

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:42.020
What impact that's going to have on you.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:43.020
I mean, I don't really know.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.090
I think it's just part of the way we all are.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:49.580
And you're going to kind of be like monkey
see, monkey do, which to a sense is fine.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:54.600
I think the real challenge comes when there
are, for example, CDC guidelines and large

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:58.590
populations of people who don't want to follow
them.

00:05:58.590 --> 00:05:59.590
Right.

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:03.020
So this is where they're kind of like monkey
see, monkey do brain is telling them, oh,

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:07.750
don't bother wearing a mask when it really
is important because there are new strains.

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:12.150
There are other kinds of considerations to
really think about.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:16.570
And so I don't really I'm not concerned about
the psychological impact of like switching

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:22.030
whether you wear masks or not is everyone's
going to be equilibria to like what's a what's

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:23.790
a healthy amount of mask wearing?

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:28.980
Speaker 1: Speaking of equilibrium, you mentioned
the significant increase in in depression

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:31.530
and reported depressive symptoms during the
pandemic.

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:36.780
I know many people who have gone on serotonin,
antidepressants, the ones that have been around

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.550
for four decades at this point in time.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:46.060
What is the medical science at this point
about the from what I looked at before our

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:51.440
conversation, it seems like a typical course
of those is often like a 12 to 18 month period.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:54.520
And then often people start kind of coming
off of those.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:07:00.419
Would it be expected that when people get
beyond this phase of the pandemic, where maybe

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:05.080
they don't feel that they need those medications
anymore, that they're going to go sort of

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:10.540
back to their pre pandemic depressive state,
or does this stuff tend to be sort of sticky

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:11.540
and persistent?

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:16.520
Speaker 6: So it's not going to be sticky
and persistent based on my professional experience,

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:22.940
I, I, I'm just going to say that I don't think
that if you have a pandemic induced depression,

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:28.030
I don't know that an SSRI is actually like
an appropriate clinical response.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:29.030
Interesting.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:34.910
So so let's remember that depression is a
symptom actually, and has multiple diagnoses

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:36.389
that can lead to depression.

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:41.530
So you can have cancer induced depression,
you can have bipolar disorder, you can have

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:46.240
major depressive disorder, you can have depressive
symptoms relating to trauma.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:52.520
And what really SSRI is have been studied
very well in is these neurochemical imbalances

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:55.000
really within major depressive disorder.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:00.680
So I would first of all, personally, I've
been hesitant to just prescribe SSRI to people

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:05.600
who are coming in for the first time depressed
during the pandemic, because we know that

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:11.440
depressive symptoms can be highly circumstantial
and really changing those circumstances is

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.570
really the permanent solution.

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:17.960
That being said, so if we kind of assume that
for a second, I wouldn't expect that the gigantic

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:22.770
crop of people who got put on our eyes will
need them going forward.

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:27.240
So I do think it's reasonable to pull off
of medication within I mean, generally speaking,

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.199
I'll try within six months to a year interest.

00:08:30.199 --> 00:08:35.099
I think 18 months is even sounds a little
bit long to me because the data suggests that

00:08:35.099 --> 00:08:37.999
a depressive episode resolves on its own within
one year.

00:08:37.999 --> 00:08:42.750
Speaker 1: Basically, I want to talk a little
bit about something that that's maybe a little

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:47.410
more in the political space and whether you
do or don't want to get involved in the political

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:48.990
commentary part is fine.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:55.309
But we've been seeing what seems to me as
a kind of coping mechanism for those who supported

00:08:55.309 --> 00:09:01.990
the former president in the election, essentially
denying that he really lost.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:09.459
And if we think of denial as maybe the first
stage in a process, sometimes in life, there

00:09:09.459 --> 00:09:13.899
seems to be sort of like an inability to move
on from the denial phase that that I'm noticing.

00:09:13.899 --> 00:09:18.079
And he's going to be back in August or maybe
it's September and this type of thing is that

00:09:18.079 --> 00:09:19.930
what do you make of that?

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:23.680
Do people sometimes never move on from the
denial phase?

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:28.339
Speaker 6: I really can't make much of that,
to be honest, because what I'd really like

00:09:28.339 --> 00:09:31.220
to do, David, is like talk to these people
so

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:32.490
Speaker 1: and see what's going on specifically.

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:33.490
Speaker 6: Yeah.

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:39.410
So, I mean, I definitely think that people
can get attached to a particular idea and

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:41.740
they have trouble letting go of that idea.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:46.810
But I think if we really want to understand
why the denial is so sticky, to use one of

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:52.970
your words, I think the real question is what
does it mean to these people that Trump did

00:09:52.970 --> 00:09:56.439
lose and what does it mean to these people
if he comes back?

00:09:56.439 --> 00:10:02.179
Because usually what I find in persistent
denial is that it's not the actual thing,

00:10:02.179 --> 00:10:04.449
it's what that thing means to the person.

00:10:04.449 --> 00:10:06.819
So the world will be a better place.

00:10:06.819 --> 00:10:10.790
And I want the world to be a better place
if X, Y or Z happens.

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:11.790
Right.

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:16.080
And so I think it's the association or the
meaning or the interpretation of the particular

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:18.040
event which makes the denial so sticky.

00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:22.879
Speaker 1: So maybe asking one of these folks,
like when they call into me it, what would

00:10:22.879 --> 00:10:27.679
it mean to you to accept that Trump genuinely
just did lose?

00:10:27.679 --> 00:10:31.980
And that might give me insight into why they're
not moving on from the denial.

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:34.929
Speaker 6: Yeah, I would modify that question
a little bit.

00:10:34.929 --> 00:10:36.500
I wouldn't say to accept.

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:37.500
What did it mean?

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:40.740
What does it mean to you if Trump lost?

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:43.529
What does it mean to you if he never comes
back?

00:10:43.529 --> 00:10:45.050
What would that say about the world?

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:46.050
What would that say?

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:50.170
Because I think what it is, is it's really
the what it says about the world is what they

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:51.560
don't want to accept.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:55.230
They don't want to live in a world where this
kind of thing happens.

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:56.230
Right.

00:10:56.230 --> 00:10:59.720
So they're going to continue hoping that this
is not the world that they live in.

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:01.110
That would be my best guess.

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:08.399
Speaker 1: If you're in your practice, do
you see people who who maybe have a depressive

00:11:08.399 --> 00:11:15.579
or an anxiety sort of a situation that is
caused by the sort of general events that

00:11:15.579 --> 00:11:19.800
are taking place in society, not the pandemic
is like a very specific medical event, but

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:25.189
sort of like what they identify as a political
problem or something along those lines.

00:11:25.189 --> 00:11:26.749
Speaker 6: Absolutely do.

00:11:26.749 --> 00:11:30.450
So like this is why, like we started Healthy
Gaymer, because I think what's happening is

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:37.069
depression used to be an isolated neurochemical
imbalance, a malfunction in the brain.

00:11:37.069 --> 00:11:45.089
But there's almost like a societal, generational
or existential depression that we see all

00:11:45.089 --> 00:11:50.300
throughout, like Gen Z and millennials, to
be honest.

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:53.779
And so, like, the world is just becoming a
bleak place.

00:11:53.779 --> 00:11:55.869
And how do people deal with that?

00:11:55.869 --> 00:11:58.470
You know, when, when, when?

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:04.759
How can I wake up as a sophomore in college
when I know that every year that goes by,

00:12:04.759 --> 00:12:10.139
I'm racking up an insurmountable amount of
debt that as I enter the job market, I'm like

00:12:10.139 --> 00:12:12.540
getting into a trench that I'm never going
to escape from.

00:12:12.540 --> 00:12:13.540
Right.

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:15.430
How can I get the motivation to study?

00:12:15.430 --> 00:12:20.489
And if you go in to see a psychiatrist, they
will check certain boxes diagnosed with depression

00:12:20.489 --> 00:12:22.170
and put you on a pill.

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:23.220
And I just don't know that.

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:24.410
I mean, can it help?

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:25.410
Absolutely.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:28.069
Could there be an underlying neurochemical
malfunction?

00:12:28.069 --> 00:12:29.069
Absolutely.

00:12:29.069 --> 00:12:34.100
But I think part of the reason that healthy
gamer has grown so much and we basically just

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:39.369
got started right after I was on your show
the last time is because we need different

00:12:39.369 --> 00:12:46.769
kinds of solutions in terms of dealing with
these different and new causes of depression

00:12:46.769 --> 00:12:47.769
like this.

00:12:47.769 --> 00:12:49.519
Existential depression is absolutely real.

00:12:49.519 --> 00:12:54.089
Speaker 1: I mean, the student loan thing,
it's almost like a capitalism based depression

00:12:54.089 --> 00:12:55.089
of sorts.

00:12:55.089 --> 00:12:56.089
Speaker 6: Yeah.

00:12:56.089 --> 00:12:59.660
I mean, so I think that this is where, like
something something about the way that our

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:06.310
personal psychology is being affected by world
events seems to be a lot tighter.

00:13:06.310 --> 00:13:10.940
So one hundred years ago, I don't know that
world events affected people's psychology

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:11.940
so much.

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:18.410
But as world events get pushed on our mind
so much, they start to have a bigger personal

00:13:18.410 --> 00:13:19.720
effect on us.

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:22.959
And that's actually part of the reason that
we started as an organization, because I don't

00:13:22.959 --> 00:13:27.680
think the old systems of psychiatry are sufficient
to deal with those kinds of things.

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:29.449
Speaker 1: No question about that.

00:13:29.449 --> 00:13:31.930
That part seems that part seems abundantly
clear.

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:36.670
A healthy gamer is the organization we've
been speaking with its co-founder, Dr. Orlock

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:41.499
Kanojia, who's a psychiatrist practicing in
Boston, Massachusetts, and also on the faculty

00:13:41.499 --> 00:13:43.360
at Harvard Medical School.

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:44.760
Always great speaking to you.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:45.760
I appreciate it.

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:47.370
Speaker 6: Thanks a lot, David, as a pleasure
